  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Creating the foundation for a more sustainable apparel industry by improving purchasing practices through a manufacturer-led approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainable Terms of Trade Initiative (STTI) is a programme set up by the International Apparel Federation and the STAR Network. It brings together 13 associations from 10 garment production countries, including Bangladesh, Cambodia, China, India, Indonesia, Morocco, Myanmar, Pakistan, T&#xfc;rkiye and Vietnam.It is set up to create a united force and a united voice of garment manufacturers with the aim of improving purchasing practices. STTI created the term &#x2018;Commercial Compliance&#x2019; that links purchasing practices to sustainability. Commercial Compliance is defined as purchasing practices that do not cause obvious and avoidable harm to manufacturers, which do not block the manufacturers&#x2019; ability to meet objectives for sustainable business.STTI has first defined what manufacturers consider to be unacceptable purchasing practices in a white paper that was published in 2021 and that has been very influential in the industry. It has then continued to work with partners in several workstreams to create instruments on a global level that support the improvement of purchasing practices.These include working with several influential MultiStakeholder Initiatives to include STTI&#x2019;s ideas of better purchasing practices in demands made to brands, working with the Responsible Contracting Project to create model contracts that reflect better purchasing practices, developing grievance mechanisms for manufacturers to complain about brands&apos; purchasing practices, gathering information about purchasing practices from manufacturers globally with partner Better Buying/Cascale and working with brands in the Netherlands in pilot projects to help them improve purchasing practices.</narrative>
  </description>
